### A. Founder dashboard

The first screen should answer:

- How many learners are registered?
- How many are actively learning?
- How many courses are published?
- How many enrollments have been made?
- How much verified revenue has been received?
- Which payments need attention?
- Which courses are performing well?
- Which learners are falling behind?
- What is scheduled next?
- Are there unresolved platform problems?

Suggested dashboard indicators:

| Metric              | What it tells the founder                                         |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Total learners      | Registered accounts                                               |
| Active learners     | Learners active during a defined period                           |
| Published courses   | Courses currently available                                       |
| Total enrollments   | Enrollment records                                                |
| Verified revenue    | Successfully confirmed payments, less applicable refunds          |
| Completion rate     | Learners completing courses against a clearly defined denominator |
| Pending reviews     | Assignments awaiting evaluation                                   |
| Upcoming programmes | Scheduled programmes and events                                   |

Do not fill the dashboard with decorative statistics. Every number must come from real records, have a clear definition and lead to a useful action.

## 4. The course builder — the heart of the platform

This is where the education business will actually operate.

The administrator should be able to prepare the entire 28-Day Practical AI Challenge without editing source code.

I recommend a guided course-building experience with a clear progression.

### A. Course management

Each course should support:

- Course title and subtitle.
- Description and learning outcomes.
- Cover image and promotional video.
- Category and difficulty level.
- Estimated duration.
- Language availability.
- Instructor assignment.
- Course price and currency.
- Free or paid access.
- Enrollment capacity, if applicable.
- Start and end dates, if applicable.
- Prerequisites.
- Certificate eligibility.
- Publication status.
- SEO title and description.
- Course visibility.

Course statuses:

(Draft) (In review) (Published) (Scheduled) (Archived)

A course should not become publicly available just because an administrator has started creating it.

### B. Curriculum builder

Use a hierarchy that mirrors how we prepare lessons.

**Example: 28-Day Practical AI Challenge** (Course structure)

**Module 1 — AI Foundations**

Days 1–7 · 7 lessons

1. Day 1 — What Is Artificial Intelligence?
2. Day 2 — AI vs ML vs Deep Learning
3. Day 3 — Generative AI and LLMs
4. Day 4 — How AI Generates Responses
5. Day 5 — Hallucinations, Bias and Fact-Checking
6. Day 6 — Responsible AI, Privacy and Ethics
7. Day 7 — Weekly Assessment

**Module 2 — Prompt Engineering**

Days 8–14 · 7 lessons

Lessons, activities and weekly assessment.

**Module 3 — Workflow Thinking and Automation**

Days 15–21 · 7 lessons

Lessons, demonstrations and practical assessment.

**Module 4 — AI-Powered Automation**

Days 22–28 · 7 lessons

Lessons, final project and assessment.

Administrators should be able to:

- Add, edit, duplicate, reorder and delete modules.
- Add lessons inside modules.
- Drag lessons into a different order.
- Mark lessons as required or optional.
- Set prerequisites and completion requirements.
- Preview the complete course as a learner.
- Save drafts without publishing.
- Schedule module or lesson releases.
- See the completion status of the course content itself.

### C. A powerful lesson editor

This is particularly important because our lessons contain more than paragraphs.

The editor should support structured content blocks:

| Content block        | Purpose                                 |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Rich text            | Explanations, headings and lists        |
| Image                | Diagrams, illustrations and screenshots |
| Video                | Recorded lessons and demonstrations     |
| Audio                | Audio learning materials                |
| Code block           | Programming examples                    |
| Callout              | Important warnings and key points       |
| Table                | Comparisons and structured information  |
| Download             | PDFs, templates and worksheets          |
| External resource    | Learning links                          |
| Quiz                 | Knowledge checks                        |
| Assignment           | Practical activities                    |
| Reflection           | Learner questions                       |
| Checklist            | Completion tasks                        |
| Interactive activity | Supported learning interactions         |

**Critical design decision:** Build a structured lesson editor, not just a giant text box.

Our Day 1, Day 2 and Day 3 materials should be convertible into organized platform lessons with headings, images, exercises, assessment questions and resources.

The editor should include:

1. English and Kinyarwanda versions.
2. Autosave and visible save status.
3. Preview mode.
4. Draft and publishing controls.
5. Lesson ordering.
6. Required/optional lesson settings.
7. Estimated completion time.
8. Learning objectives.
9. Attachments and downloadable materials.
10. Version history and restoration.

### D. English and Kinyarwanda content management

Every course and lesson needs a shared identity with language-specific content.

For example:

**Lesson: What Is Artificial Intelligence?**

English (Published)

English title, body, exercises and resources.

---

Kinyarwanda (Translation needed)

Separate Kinyarwanda title, body, exercises and resources.

Administrators should be able to:

- Switch between language versions.
- See missing translations.
- Copy an English draft into a Kinyarwanda draft as a starting point.
- Edit each language independently.
- Publish one language without accidentally publishing an unfinished translation.
- Track translation completion.

Never automatically publish an AI-generated translation without human review.

### E. Course publishing checklist

Before publishing, the system should check:

- Course title and description exist.
- Learning objectives exist.
- At least one module exists.
- Required lessons have content.
- Required translations are ready for the selected language.
- Pricing and enrollment settings are valid.
- Instructor assignment is valid, if required.
- Certificate rules are configured, if a certificate is offered.
- Course preview is available.

The system should explain what is missing instead of simply displaying an error.

